Derive a batch operation from interface guarantees (@computing-abstraction-composition: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-behavioral-substitutability-from-cost-and-changed-requirements.lingua#r_0093E00EA98E496E9BC6C23AA1") { r_00B5EDC9D78BFFA550AB83560D
Build a client of the slot interface without reading its fields. `reserve_many(state,requests)` receives a valid state (m,S) and an exact tuple of distinct exact slot integers. First validate the complete tuple. If it is malformed, raise ValueError. For a valid request set R, if C=S∩R is nonempty return the unchanged abstract state and ('conflict',min C). Otherwise return a state for (m,S∪R) and ('accepted',|R|). Request order cannot change the abstract result or selected conflict. An empty request is accepted with count zero.

The implementation first checks types, ranges and repetitions, then observes all conflicts, then calculates a new result only when none exists. This ordering establishes the malformed-input priority. The selected minimum conflict is an explicit deterministic rule; returning the first encountered conflict would give a different observation for reordered requests. No caller state is mutated in any case.

On the successful path, after k insertions the accumulating result represents S∪{r_0,...,r_{k−1}}. The interface's insertion law proves preservation. The finite request length proves termination under the resource assumptions. After the last insertion, the required union is obtained. The original state is still S because all intermediate values are immutable under the permitted operations. On the conflict path there is no proposal update; the result remains S and the reported member belongs to C and is its minimum. The proof uses interface laws, so it applies to either representation.

For m=5, S={3}, and requests (1,3), the correct result is the original set with conflict 3. A rival that accumulates insertions and returns its intermediate candidate when it sees a conflict instead returns {1,3}. Its conflict label is correct but its state result is wrong. Checking only the status would miss the violation. A rival that stops validation when it sees an occupied slot also mishandles a later malformed value.

This local all-or-conflict result is not a transaction involving a physical resource. Two separate callers starting from the same old value can each calculate acceptance for the same slot. Committing a unique shared reservation needs a different coordination/persistence contract, connected to [Review concurrent state safety progress and observation limits](Review-concurrent-state-safety-progress-and-observation-limits.lingua#r_002962E53DB9AFD36AEA18CA3C) and [Review communication contracts identity and conditional completion](Review-communication-contracts-identity-and-conditional-completion.lingua#r_00AEE3B68FE4F0253687A55642). No network or storage guarantee follows from returning an immutable proposal.

Use the shared module lesson to separate decoding, abstract validation, proposal calculation, rendering and external commitment. A textual adapter owns its units and syntax; the pure operation owns the set behavior. Changing the renderer must not quietly change conflict choice or failure interpretation. Importing the study module defines functions/classes and creates no booking or report.

**Work:** derive all three branches and the successful-loop invariant. Test a reordered multi-conflict request and a conflict followed by invalid input. Identify the exact observation distinguishing the incremental rival. Design the additional interface needed for shared commitment without pretending the local proposal already supplies it.

**Continue:** [Separate behavioral substitutability from cost and changed requirements](Separate-behavioral-substitutability-from-cost-and-changed-requirements.lingua#r_0093E00EA98E496E9BC6C23AA1).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
